<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Trenčín</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Trencsén</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Trenčín is a city in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a> on the Váh river, overlooked by one of the largest castles in the country. Known in Hungarian as Trencsén, it was the stronghold of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnate</a> Matthew Csák, who ruled much of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> from it in the early 14th century. A Roman inscription of 179 CE on the castle rock, recording a legion's winter camp at Laugaricio, is the northernmost Roman monument in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-europe">Central Europe</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/trencin.jpg" alt="Trenčín" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Abphoto · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Trencin_hdr_001.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Trenčín lies in the central valley of the Váh River in the Trenčín Basin, near the Czech border and about 95 kilometres from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bratislava">Bratislava</a>, at an altitude of 217 metres. The basin is surrounded by the Strážov Mountains, Považský Inovec and the White Carpathians. It was first mentioned under the Greek name Leukaristos on a world map based on Ptolemy, around 150 CE, and the Roman inscription of 179 CE names the place as Laugaricio. The medieval name probably derives from a personal name, Trnka or Trenka, meaning blackthorn, with a possessive suffix, and the first medieval mentions date from 1111 and 1113.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Trenčín is one of the suggested locations for the capital of Samo's Empire in the 7th century, and Trenčín Castle may have been founded in the Great Moravian era. In 1017 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stephen-i-of-hungary">Stephen I of Hungary</a> conquered the region, which stayed part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> until 1918. By the end of the 11th century the castle was the administrative centre of Trencsén county. As one of the few stone castles in the country it resisted the Mongol invasion of 1241. Between 1302 and 1321 it was the seat of the magnate Matthew Csák, who controlled most of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a> and challenged King Charles Robert. The Treaty of Trentschin between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, Hungary and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> was signed in the city in 1335.</p><p>The town gained freedom from tolls in 1324 and free royal town status from King Sigismund in 1412. Imperial troops captured it in 1528 during the conflict between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> and the supporters of János Szapolyai. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> failed to take it in the 17th century. The Battle of Trenčín was fought near the city on 3 August 1708, and a plague killed 1,600 inhabitants two years later. In 1790 the town and castle burned down, and the castle has been a ruin since. The 19th century brought railways and new industries.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Trenčín is the seat of the Trenčín Region and District and has more than 55,000 inhabitants. Trenčín Castle, the third largest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a>, stands on a rock above the city, with its upper castle and central medieval tower. The old town has a large main square, a baroque church and a town tower. The Roman inscription on the rock below the castle, carved in 179 CE during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcomannic-wars">Marcomannic Wars</a>, is its best-known monument. The historical centre has been largely restored since 1990.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Trenčín” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Marcus Aurelius made his headquarters at Carnuntum, and in 179 Marcus Valerius Maximianus won a decisive battle at Laugaricio (<b>Trenčín</b>).</blockquote><a href="/empire/marcomanni">Marcomanni · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After the death of Andrew III in 1301 ended the Árpád line, he backed rival claimants and seized castles across north-western Hungary, centred on <b>Trencsén</b> (Trenčín).</blockquote><a href="/state/domain-of-matthew-csak">Domain of Matthew Csák</a></li></ul></section>
